So: the worker is the candidate's bytes at every commit. Main's own work between the candidate and this branch changed
the cold stage and nothing of the other parts, in text or in bytes. The first import changed the text of retail_async.c,
sci_stream.c and native_readahead_aux.c as this recipe compiles them, and the auxiliary's bytes with it (the low
residents' bytes stayed). The second changed those and sci_sd_bus.c and vmu_native_background_resident.c, and the bytes
of both low residents and the auxiliary. Nothing since has changed any of the 23 texts: the stream reader's Tests 68 to
82 are behind switches this recipe does not set, and it builds the same module at e1104f4 and at this source.

What the two imports changed in those texts (diff of the preprocessed sources, candidate against this source): a counter
of the stream's shared record given a second name in a union; statements split over lines; in sci_stream.c the framing
path's three returns turned into jumps to one exit, and the enumerator DMA written with its value; in retail_async.c a
local for the visit's fourth argument, the delivery's test turned round, the read-ahead record's size assert split in
two; in native_readahead_aux.c a wrapper round the demand delivery, the block cache's size as the constant 384 * 512
where the candidate passes the configured size, the guard's operands in another order. By the text these are the Toy
suite's hooks folded into shared sources with nothing in them for this recipe. Whether the compiled differences change
what the parts do has not been examined, and no console has run them.

Test 82 takes the worker from this recipe and nothing else of it. Its low residents and its auxiliary are the Toy
tests' (Test 81's), built with the Toy switches from the same sources. Owed before the installed recipe is built from
this source for anything: either the recipe's own configuration is brought back to the candidate's bytes, or what
differs is reviewed and run on a console as a change of its own. Until then the installed New reader is the delivered
candidate's file, and its recipe reproduces it at the candidate's source alone.
